 <h2>Introduction</h2>
 <p>Choosing between a robo-advisor and DIY investing is less about finding a universally “best” option and more about deciding how much control and responsibility you want. A robo-advisor can automate portfolio construction and rebalancing. DIY investing can give you more control and may reduce advisory costs, but you must choose investments and manage the account yourself.</p>
 <p>The right choice depends on your goals, investing knowledge, time, risk tolerance, account type, and ability to stay disciplined during market declines. This guide compares both approaches without recommending a particular platform or investment.</p>
